# ── Marketplace OAuth landing ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# THERE IS NO RULE HERE ANYMORE, ON PURPOSE. This used to hold two `202`
# "proxy" lines on the theory that a 202 forwards the request.
# Cloudflare Pages only parses redirect statuses (200/301/302/303/307/308).
# 202 is on the docs' list of rules that are parsed and silently ignored —
# the same list, and the same failure mode, as the /404 asset lines described
# at the bottom of this file. So neither line ever did anything, and every
# seller returning from the eBay, Etsy or Whatnot consent screen fell through
# to the static 404 page with `state` still in the query string. Verified:
#   curl -I https://fashionistas.ai/api/channels/callback        -> 404
#   curl -I https://fashionistas.ai/api/channels/callback/etsy   -> 404
#   (both while these rules were present)
# The proxy now lives where it is guaranteed to run, ahead of both the asset
# layer and _redirects — functions/api/channels/callback/[[channel]].js.
# The two `202` lines that used to sit here were deleted rather than left as
# documentation: they never matched anything, and a rule nobody can see fail is
# how this broke the first time.

# ── Internal files: never served publicly ────────────────────────────────────
# The Pages output dir is the repo root, so anything checked in would otherwise
# ship as a public URL.
#
# WHY THE OLD "… /404.html 404" RULES WERE REMOVED:
# Cloudflare Pages does not support rewrites with a status code that is not a
# redirect. Its docs list "Rewrites (other status codes)" as unsupported and
# use `/blog/* /blog/404.html 404` as the literal example of a rule that is
# parsed and silently ignored. Because of that these lines never shadowed an
# asset that exists in the build output — /wrangler.toml, /package.json,
# /NEEDS_ISRAEL.txt and /docs/*.md were all served as 200 while they were here.
# (Rules such as /.env, /libs or /wrangler-d1.toml only *looked* effective:
# those files are simply not deployed, so the 404 came from the asset layer.)
#
# REAL ENFORCEMENT → Pages Functions. Functions are matched before the static
# asset layer, so they win even when the file ships in the deploy:
#   functions/_lib/blocked.js        shared 404 responder (site's own 404 body)
#   functions/wrangler.toml.js       functions/package.json.js
#   functions/NEEDS_ISRAEL.txt.js    functions/TEST.md.js
#   functions/docs/[[path]].js       functions/libs/[[path]].js
#   functions/scripts/[[path]].js    functions/apps/[[path]].js
#   functions/core/[[path]].js       functions/.github/[[path]].js
#   functions/.env.js  functions/.env.example.js  functions/.dev.vars.js
#   functions/wrangler-d1.toml.js    functions/package-lock.json.js
#
# The /…  /__blocked__  200 lines below are belt-and-braces for the asset layer
# only: they are consulted solely when a path is served as a static asset
# instead of by a Function (for example if a route were excluded through
# _routes.json). /__blocked__ has no asset, so the internal file's contents are
# replaced by the site's 404 page rather than being served. Functions take
# precedence over _redirects, so these never interfere with the routes above.
/wrangler.toml            /__blocked__   200
/package.json             /__blocked__   200
/NEEDS_ISRAEL.txt         /__blocked__   200
/TEST.md                  /__blocked__   200
/.env.example             /__blocked__   200
/docs/*                   /__blocked__   200
/libs/*                   /__blocked__   200
/scripts/*                /__blocked__   200
/apps/*                   /__blocked__   200
/core/*                   /__blocked__   200
/.github/*                /__blocked__   200
